  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1006 PM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThe subtropical moisture along and S of the Int'l border will\ndrift further into MX for Tuesday as our weak upper high is \nreplaced by a weak low over Utah. Less cloud cover is forecast\nwith no chance of rain in the short term. Light winds with near-\nnormal temps for Tue. \n\nThe upper low expands SE for Wed, creating a bit of forcing and very\nslight rain/storm chances in area mtns. Meager moisture will limit\nrainfall where weak convection does develop. A weak Rex block\nlooks to form for Thu across the Four Corners, pushing the low\ntowards the Big Bend region. Moisture levels remain modest into\nlate week with PWs in the 0.7-0.8\" range, which is enough to spark\na few showers or storms in the Sacs on Thu. Gusty winds and\nlightning are the main hazards as the lowlands stay dry.\n\nThe upper trough looks to weaken and drift over South Texas into \nthe weekend with not much lift or moisture to spark showers or\nstorms Fri/Sat. Early next week, a mid-latitude trough is progged\nto move through the Great Basin, scooping up moisture ahead of it\nlate in the period. Low-end rain chances return next Sun/Mon, \nfavoring western areas. We'll see exactly how windy/wet it'll be \ndepending on the track of the low through the Rockies. The low\nshould also bring cooler air around the middle of next week. Until\nthen, temps will be around normal with light winds continuing.",
